et), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Hand Analysis: River Decision**\n\nOkay, here's the situation. I'm deep into this heads-up match, hand 19 of 20. I've got a decent stack (19,900), and I'm currently leading by 550 chips. My cards are King of Clubs, Ten of Diamonds. The board is brutal: Ace-Ace-Queen-Two-Two. My best hand is a solid two pair, Aces and Twos, with a King kicker. \n\nMy opponent has checked all the way through, pre-flop, flop, and turn. That's the key. Their range is probably quite wide and weak, but could be a trap. \n\nMy immediate thoughts are, what beats me? Obviously, any Ace, any Queen, any Two, and any pair higher than Twos beats me. I beat pure air, or hands that have just a lower kicker than mine. But the board is paired, so it's likely they have something. I have to play this carefully. With the board paired, I lose to a lot of hands.\n\nGiven the history and board, betting doesn't seem to make much sense. I'm ahead right now, and I want to preserve that lead. My hand has showdown value because the King plays as a kicker, but I don't want to get into a bluffing war here. Betting would just invite trouble from hands that beat me and I can't be sure what that is.\n\nThe prudent move is to check. Checking allows me to get to a cheap showdown, where I have a good chance to win or chop the pot, and minimize the risk of losing my chip lead. I'm thinking a safe path to a potential win with my kicker, and protect the lead, is the right strategy here.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: Your hand is Two Pair (Aces and Twos) with a King kicker (A-A-2-2-K). The board is A-A-Q-2-2. Your hand beats playing the board (which uses the Queen kicker) and beats any opponent holding a high card lower than a King (e.g., J-high). However, you lose to any player holding a Pocket Pair (33+), an Ace, a Queen, or a Two. Since the opponent has checked on the Pre-flop, Flop, and Turn, they are likely weak, but betting serves 